 New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Proof of 2.1 Building the ℓ 1 \ell^{1} example 2.2 The coloring 2.3 Projecting to ℤ \mathbb{Z} 3 Concluding Remarks and Open Questions 4 Acknowledgments References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2511.21680v3 [math.DS] 24 Dec 2025 New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence Ryan Alweiss † † thanks: Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics and Trinity College, University of Cambridge. Email: ra699@cam.ac.uk . Research supported by an NSF Mathematical Sciences Postdoctoral Fellowship. Abstract We show that there is a set which is not a set of multiple recurrence despite being a set of recurrence for nil-Bohr sets. This answers Huang, Shao, and Ye’s “higher-order” version of Katznelson’s Question on Bohr recurrence and topological recurrence in the negative. Equivalently, we construct a set S S so that there is a finite coloring of ℕ \mathbb{N} without three-term arithmetic progressions with common differences in S S , but so that S S lacks the usual polynomial obstacles to arithmetic progressions. 1 Introduction A central and long-standing open problem in the field of topological dynamics is “Katznelson’s Question” , on whether a set of Bohr recurrence is a set of topological recurrence. This question was raised by a 1968 paper of Veech [ 21 ] and implicitly before that in a 1954 paper of Følner [ 6 ] . However, in a classic example of Stigler’s law, it is often referred to as “Katznelson’s question” , because Katznelson popularized the question and wrote a beautiful paper [ 17 ] connecting it to the chromatic number of abelian Cayley graphs. In this paper, via a Furstenberg correspondence principle type argument in symbolic dynamics, he shows that Bohr recurrence is equivalent to topological recurrence if and only if every abelian Cayley graph with finite chromatic number has a finite coloring via Bohr sets. Let us recall the equivalent dynamical and combinatorial definitions of Bohr and topological recurrence, and some basics of topological dynamics. These appear widely in the literature (see for instance [ 17 ] [ 10 ] [ 7 ] ). Recall that ‖ x ‖ ℝ / ℤ = min ⁡ ( { x } , 1 − { x } ) ||x||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}=\min(\{x\},1-\{x\}) is the distance between x x and the nearest integer. Definition 1.1 (Bohr recurrence, combinatorial definition) . A set S ⊂ ℤ S\subset\mathbb{Z} is a set of Bohr recurrence if and only if for all nonzero α 1 , ⋯ , α k ∈ ℝ \alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k}\in\mathbb{R} and all ε > 0 \varepsilon>0 there exists some s ∈ S s\in S so that for all 1 ≤ i ≤ k 1\leq i\leq k , ‖ α i ​ s ‖ ℝ / ℤ < ε ||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}<\varepsilon . We will also formally define a Bohr set. Definition 1.2 (Bohr set) . A Bohr set is a set S S parameterized by a k k -tuple of reals ( α 1 , ⋯ , α k ) (\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k}) and ε \varepsilon , consisting of the s s so that ‖ α i ​ s ‖ ℝ / ℤ < ε ||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}<\varepsilon for all 1 ≤ i ≤ k 1\leq i\leq k . The parameter k k is called the dimension and ε \varepsilon is the width . Later we will define a complexity of a nil-Bohr set, which will be Θ ⁡ ( max ⁡ ( k , 1 / ε ) ) \Theta(\max(k,1/\varepsilon)) for ordinary Bohr sets. Equivalently a set is a set of Bohr recurrence if it is not contained in the complement of a Bohr set, or equivalently in a finite union of complements of one-dimensional Bohr sets. Sometimes this is referred to as a “ B ​ o ​ h ​ r 0 Bohr_{0} set” in the literature, with the term Bohr set referring to the more general class of translates of B ​ o ​ h ​ r 0 Bohr_{0} sets. Here we will only deal with B ​ o ​ h ​ r 0 Bohr_{0} sets, so we will refer to them simply as Bohr sets. Definition 1.3 (Bohr recurrence, dynamical definition) . A set S ⊂ ℤ S\subset\mathbb{Z} is a set of Bohr recurrence if and only if for all k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} and all rotations T ⁡ ( x 1 , ⋯ , x k ) = ( x 1 + α 1 , ⋯ , x k + α k ) T(x_{1},\cdots,x_{k})=(x_{1}+\alpha_{1},\cdots,x_{k}+\alpha_{k}) on the torus ( ℝ / ℤ ) k (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k} , there exists a point x ∈ ( ℝ / ℤ ) k x\in(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k} and a subsequence S ′ S^{\prime} of n ∈ S n\in S so that lim n ∈ S ′ T n ​ ( x ) = x \lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}(x)=x . The two definitions are readily seen to be equivalent. Since torus rotations are isometries, if T n ​ x → x T^{n}x\to x is true for a single point then it is true for all points, so it would be equivalent to change the quantifier on x x from “there exists” to “for all” . We only consider S S with 0 ∉ S 0\notin S , as if 0 ∈ S 0\in S then all notions of recurrence are trivial. In general dealing with S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is equivalent to dealing with S ∪ − S ⊂ ℤ S\cup-S\subset\mathbb{Z} , so we will assume for the remainder of this paper that S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} . This is important, because a general topological system is not necessarily invertible, so T − 1 T^{-1} may not make sense. Recall that if S S is a subset of a group G G , the Cayley graph C ​ a ​ y ​ ( G , S ) Cay(G,S) on an abelian group G G has all elements of G G as vertices and a , b a,b in G G are adjacent if and only if a − b ∈ ( S ∪ − S ) a-b\in(S\cup-S) . Recall also that the chromatic number of a graph is the minimal number of colors needed to color the vertices so that no two vertices of the same color are adjacent. Definition 1.4 (Topological recurrence, combinatorial definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of topological recurrence if and only if the Cayley graph C ​ a ​ y ​ ( ℤ , S ) Cay(\mathbb{Z},S) on ℤ \mathbb{Z} generated by S S has infinite chromatic number. Topological recurrence is the most general form of recurrence, applying to all dynamical systems. Definition 1.5 (Topological recurrence, dynamical definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of topological recurrence if for every compact dynamical system ( X , T ) (X,T) , where X X is a compact metric space and T T is a continuous map on X X , there exists a point x ∈ X x\in X and a subsequence S ′ S^{\prime} of S S so that lim n ∈ S ′ T n ​ x = x \lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}x=x . It was Katznelson [ 17 ] who proved the equivalence of these two definitions of topological recurrence, giving the eponymous question its combinatorial form. One direction is relatively simple. If a set S S is not a set of dynamical topological recurrence, then one can use the dynamical system to come up with a coloring of the Cayley graph. In particular there will be some ε \varepsilon so that d ⁡ ( T n ​ x , x ) > ε d(T^{n}x,x)>\varepsilon for all n ∈ S n\in S . Now cover the compact space by ε \varepsilon -balls for a suitably small ε \varepsilon , and color m ∈ ℕ m\in\mathbb{N} by which ε \varepsilon -ball T m ​ ( x ) T^{m}(x) ends up in. The other direction, using a coloring to construct a dynamical system, follows from an argument of Katznelson that makes use of symbolic dynamics, and bears some resemblance to the Furstenberg correspondence principle. A set of topological recurrence must be a set of Bohr recurrence. This is readily seen both from the topological definition, as a circle rotation is a special kind of dynamical system, and from the combinatorial version, as if S S a set that is not a set of Bohr recurrence it is easy to construct a finite coloring by taking all of the n ​ α i n\alpha_{i} modulo 1 1 , up to an error of ε \varepsilon . Hence we can state Katznelson’s question explicitly as follows. Question 1.6 (Katznelson’s question) . If S S is a set of Bohr recurrence, then is S S necessarily a set of topological recurrence? In [ 7 ] it is stated that there is “no consensus among experts as to the expected answer” . No discussion of the history of Katznelson’s question would be complete without also referencing the “Related Problem” . Bergelson asked whether there is a set which is a set of topological recurrence but not a set of measurable recurrence, or equivalently whether Cayley graph on the integers with a positive upper density independent set, but infinite chromatic number, and Kříž [ 18 ] resolved this question in the negative. His construction is based on the boolean Cayley graph over ( ℤ / 2 ​ ℤ ) n (\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})^{n} generated by elements of Hamming weight > n − 10 ​ n >n-10\sqrt{n} . One can check that elements of Hamming weight ≤ n / 2 − 10 ​ n \leq n/2-10\sqrt{n} form a positive density independent set in this graph. Furthermore, looking at the elements of Hamming weight n / 2 − 6 ​ n n/2-6\sqrt{n} , this graph has a copy of the Kneser graph K ​ G ​ ( n , n / 2 − 6 ​ n ) KG(n,n/2-6\sqrt{n}) and thus has chromatic number at least n \sqrt{n} , and goes to infinity with n n . Kříž projects this construction to the integers. See [ 12 ] for an exposition of his example by Griesmer and [ 11 ] for an extension. In this paper, we will be concerned with the natural higher-order version of Katznelson’s question. This was first explicitly asked by Huang, Shao, and Ye [ 15 ] , is the main subject of their book [ 16 ] , and was asked again as Question 7 in [ 7 ] . Before stating it, we need to define topological multiple recurrence. Definition 1.7 (Topological multiple recurrence, dynamical definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of topological d d -recurrence if for every compact dynamical system ( X , T ) (X,T) , where X X is a compact metric space and T T is a continuous map on X X , there exists a point x ∈ X x\in X and a subsequence S ′ S^{\prime} of S S so that for all 1 ≤ d ′ ≤ d 1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d , lim n ∈ S ′ T d ′ ​ n ​ x = x \lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x=x . In other words, T n ​ x , T 2 ​ n ​ x , ⋯ , T d ​ n ​ x T^{n}x,T^{2n}x,\cdots,T^{dn}x all go to x x along this sequence S ′ S^{\prime} of n n . So we have T n x → x , T 2 ​ n x → x , ⋯ , T d ​ n x → x T^{n}x\to x,T^{2n}x\to x,\cdots,T^{dn}x\to x simultaneously along this sequence S ′ S^{\prime} . For d = 1 d=1 , this is the ordinary notion of recurrence. Definition 1.8 (Topological multiple recurrence, combinatorial definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of topological d d -recurrence if for every finite coloring of ℕ \mathbb{N} , there is a monochromatic ( d + 1 ) (d+1) -term arithmetic progression with common difference in S S . Equivalently, the ( d + 1 ) (d+1) -hypergraph with edges ( x , x + s , ⋯ , x + d ​ s ) (x,x+s,\cdots,x+ds) for s ∈ S s\in S has infinite chromatic number. In other words, restricted van der Waerden with differences in S S is true for ( d + 1 ) (d+1) -term arithmetic progressions. For d = 1 d=1 this is readily seen to coincide with the Cayley graph definition. As before, this combinatorial definition is equivalent to the dynamical one, see for instance [ 14 ] , Theorem 2.5. In [ 16 ] , the notation ℱ B ​ i ​ r d \mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}} is used for the family of sets of topological d d -recurrence. For restricted van der Waerden, on top of the usual obstacles in Katznelson’s question, there are higher degree obstacles. If s s is far from a multiple of an irrational number like π \pi , then x , x + s , x + 2 ​ s x,x+s,x+2s cannot all be too close modulo π \pi . If 2 ​ s 2 2s^{2} is far from a multiple of π \pi , then because x 2 − 2 ​ ( x + s ) 2 + ( x + 2 ​ s ) 2 = 2 ​ s 2 x^{2}-2(x+s)^{2}+(x+2s)^{2}=2s^{2} , the squares of x , x + s , x + 2 ​ s x,x+s,x+2s cannot all be very close to each other modulo π \pi as well, which yields a finite coloring where x x is colored by x 2 x^{2} modulo π \pi up to a small error. In general there will be polynomial obstacles up to degree d d . So the natural question here is whether there are any other obstacles, with the caveat that one has to also worry about “generalized polynomials” or “bracket polynomials” like s ​ ⌊ s ⌋ s\lfloor s\rfloor . These generalized polynomials are formally defined in [ 16 ] , but we will not define them yet. In the dynamics literature, these correspond to nilmanifolds. Multiple recurrence for Bohr sets is equivalent to single recurrence, but there is a higher-order notion of Bohr recurrence called “nil-Bohr recurrence” . It was originally defined dynamically in [ 13 ] and is the main object of study in [ 16 ] . We state it here for completeness. For a formal definition of a ( d d -step) nilsystem, one can for instance consult [ 16 ] , but again it is not so crucial for the present paper. Definition 1.9 (nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, dynamical definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence if for every d d -step nil-system ( X , T ) (X,T) there is a point x ∈ X x\in X and a subsequence S ′ ⊂ S S^{\prime}\subset S so that for all 1 ≤ d ′ ≤ d 1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d , x = lim n ∈ S ′ T d ′ ​ n ​ x x=\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x As usual, there is also a combinatorial definition. Again, we state it here for completeness. Definition 1.10 (nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, combinatorial definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence if for every finite family of generalized polynomials P 1 , ⋯ , P k P_{1},\cdots,P_{k} with degree at most d d and every ε \varepsilon , there exist s ∈ S s\in S so that ‖ P i ​ ( s ) ‖ ℝ / ℤ < ε ||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}<\varepsilon for all 1 ≤ i ≤ k 1\leq i\leq k . The set of n n so that these generalized polynomials have small ℝ / ℤ \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z} is called a “Nil-Bohr” set, or a “ N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr” set when we want to emphasize the parameter of d d . An ordinary Bohr set is just a N ​ i ​ l 1 Nil_{1} -Bohr set. The equivalence of the definitions is Theorem B of [ 16 ] , where the notation ℱ d , 0 \mathcal{F}_{d,0} is used for the family of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr sets and ℱ d , 0 ∗ \mathcal{F}_{d,0}^{*} for the family of sets of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence. Again, we will not formally define “generalized polynomial” , but it is essentially just a polynomial with floors and ceilings, often also referred to as a “bracket polynomial” in the literature (see for instance [ 16 ] , [ 20 ] ). The “degree” of a generalized polynomial is just the degree in s s ignoring the floors and ceilings, so for instance s ​ ⌊ s ⌋ s\lfloor s\rfloor will have degree 2 2 . Huang, Shao, and Ye also prove that one can also equivalently ask for the generalized polynomials P i P_{i} to be “special” generalized polynomials of an even simpler form. We will define these. First, following Section 4 of [ 16 ] we use the notation [ x ] := ⌊ x + 1 / 2 ⌋ [x]:=\lfloor x+1/2\rfloor for the nearest integer function. Definition 1.11 (Special Generalized Polynomials, after [ 16 ] ) . We define L L so that L ⁡ ( x ) = x L(x)=x and L ⁡ ( x 1 , … , x ℓ ) = x 1 ​ [ L ⁡ ( x 2 , … , x ℓ ) ] . L(x_{1},\dots,x_{\ell})=x_{1}[L(x_{2},\dots,x_{\ell})]. Then the special generalized polynomials of degree at most d d are the expressions L ⁡ ( n j 1 ​ a 1 , … , n j ℓ ​ a ℓ ) L(n^{j_{1}}a_{1},\dots,n^{j_{\ell}}a_{\ell}) such that j i ∈ ℕ j_{i}\in\mathbb{N} and a i ∈ ℝ a_{i}\in\mathbb{R} for 1 ≤ i ≤ ℓ 1\leq i\leq\ell , and ∑ i = 1 ℓ j i ≤ d \sum_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}\leq d . In particular, we must have ℓ ≤ d \ell\leq d . Definition 1.12 (nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, simple combinatorial definition) . A set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} is a set of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence if for every finite family of special generalized polynomials P 1 , ⋯ , P k P_{1},\cdots,P_{k} with degree at most d d and every ε \varepsilon , there exist s ∈ S s\in S so that ‖ P i ​ ( s ) ‖ ℝ / ℤ < ε ||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}<\varepsilon for all 1 ≤ i ≤ k 1\leq i\leq k . We have for instance that the set of n n so that 1 / 4 < { n 2 ​ α } < 3 / 4 1/4<\{n^{2}\alpha\}<3/4 and the set of n n so that 1 / 4 < { α ​ n ​ [ n ] } 1/4<\{\alpha n[n]\} are not sets of N ​ i ​ l 2 Nil_{2} -Bohr recurrence, even though they are sets of Bohr recurrence. The equivalence of the simple and ordinary combinatorial definitions of nil-Bohr multiple recurrence is Theorem 4.2.11 of [ 16 ] . In our proof, we will instead use a somewhat different form of “special generalized polynomial” from [ 20 ] . Leibman proves in his paper that these have the property that they jointly equi-distribute. The natural higher-order analogue of Katznelson’s question is whether topological multiple recurrence is equivalent to nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, and specifically whether topological d d -recurrence is equivalent to N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence. This is Question B, the main question asked in [ 16 ] , which in formulation B-III asks whether ℱ B ​ i ​ r d = ℱ d , 0 ∗ \mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}}=\mathcal{F}^{*}_{d,0} . In this paper, we answer this question in the negative. Theorem 1.13 . There is a set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} which is not a set of topological 2 2 -recurrence, but is a set of N ​ i ​ l 2 Nil_{2} -Bohr recurrence. We really only need that it is a set of N ​ i ​ l 2 Nil_{2} -Bohr recurrence, but it turns out to be no more difficult to also get this for d > 2 d>2 . The same proof works. Theorem 1.14 . There is a set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} which is not a set of topological 2 2 -recurrence, but is a set of N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr recurrence for every d d . Equivalently, we find a set S S so that restricted van der Waerden with common difference s ∈ S s\in S is false even for three-term progressions, but S S intersects all N ​ i ​ l d Nil_{d} -Bohr sets, and so it does not have quadratic obstacles like { s : ‖ s 2 ​ α ‖ ℝ / ℤ > ε } \{s:||s^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}>\varepsilon\} (or higher degree obstacles like { s : ‖ s 3 ​ α ‖ ℝ / ℤ > ε } \{s:||s^{3}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}>\varepsilon\} ) that would give an “obvious obstruction” . 2 Proof of Theorem 1.14 To prove Theorem 1.14 we must ultimately construct a set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} . However, we will first construct a set S S in a different setting, and consider an appropriate projection. Recall that ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ , ℝ / ℤ ) \ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) consists of sequences ( a 1 , a 2 , a 3 , ⋯ ) (a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots) of elements of ℝ / ℤ \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z} so that the ℓ 1 \ell^{1} norm ∑ i = 1 ∞ ‖ a i ‖ ℝ / ℤ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} is less than ∞ \infty . This is an abelian group endowed with the usual addition operation. The key fact about this group ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ , ℝ / ℤ ) \ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) that we will exploit is that it is not a Hilbert space and so has no true “quadratic structure” (and similarly, no true cubic structure or quartic structure and so on). While one can define the square of the ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm to be ∑ i = 1 ∞ ‖ a i ‖ ℝ / ℤ 2 \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||^{2}_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} which will be bounded by the usual ℓ 1 \ell^{1} norm ∑ i = 1 ∞ ‖ a i ‖ ℝ / ℤ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} (and similarly one can define other ℓ p \ell^{p} norms), one can check that the parallelogram law ‖ x + d ‖ ℓ 2 2 − 2 ​ ‖ x ‖ ℓ 2 2 + ‖ x − d ‖ ℓ 2 2 = 2 | | d | | ℓ 2 2 ||x+d||^{2}_{\ell^{2}}-2||x||^{2}_{\ell_{2}}+||x-d||_{\ell_{2}}^{2}=2||d||^{2}_{\ell_{2}} does not hold. For instance one can set x = ( 0 , 1 / 3 , 2 / 3 , 0 , ⋯ ) x=(0,1/3,2/3,0,\cdots) and d = ( 1 / 3 , 1 / 3 , 1 / 3 , 0 , ⋯ ) d=(1/3,1/3,1/3,0,\cdots) . Then ‖ d ‖ 2 2 = 1 / 3 ||d||^{2}_{2}=1/3 while ‖ x − d ‖ 2 = ‖ x ‖ 2 = | | x + d | | 2 ||x-d||_{2}=||x||_{2}=||x+d||_{2} . However, this “fake quadratic structure” from this ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm and from the curvature of the circle will enable us to come up with a coloring that prevents three-term monochromatic progressions. Over the integers, the ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm will end up corresponding to something like an infinite sum of squares of fractional parts ∑ { n ​ a i } 2 \sum\{na_{i}\}^{2} which is an infinite-dimensional generalized polynomial and does not simplify to a low-dimensional generalized polynomial like { n 2 ​ α } \{n^{2}\alpha\} where α = ∑ a i 2 \alpha=\sum a_{i}^{2} . We now describe the construction. 2.1 Building