2 \delta_{2} . Color ( a 1 , ⋯ , a m ) ∈ ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ ≤ m , ℝ / ℤ ) (a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) by the set that contains ∑ i = 1 m ( e ⁡ ( a i ) − 1 ) \sum_{i=1}^{m}(e(a_{i})-1) taken modulo ℤ ⁡ [ i ] \mathbb{Z}[i] . We now show that our coloring lacks three-term monochromatic progressions with common difference in S S . Theorem 2.6 . Let x , s ∈ ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ ≤ m , ℝ / ℤ ) x,s\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) with s ∈ S m s\in S_{m} . Then if ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ ≤ m , ℝ / ℤ ) \ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) is colored as in Definition 2.5 , then x x , x + s x+s , and x + 2 ​ s x+2s will not all receive the same color. Before proving this, we recall a basic fact from geometry and trigonometry. If we consider three equally spaced points on the unit circle, if they cut out an arc which is about a δ \delta proportion of the circle, then the middle point will have distance about δ 2 \delta^{2} from the midpoint of the arc, by the standard Taylor approximation 1 − cos ⁡ ( δ ) = δ 2 / 2 + O ⁡ ( δ 3 ) 1-\cos(\delta)=\delta^{2}/2+O(\delta^{3}) . This distance is called the “sagitta” . So the contribution to f ⁡ ( x ) − 2 ​ f ​ ( x + s ) + f ⁡ ( x + 2 ​ s ) f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s) from the big i i term so that a i ≈ δ 1 a_{i}\approx\delta_{1} will be about δ 1 2 \delta_{1}^{2} , whereas the other contributions from the j j terms will in total at most δ 1 δ 2 ​ δ 2 2 ≈ δ 1 ​ δ 2 \frac{\delta_{1}}{\delta_{2}}\delta^{2}_{2}\approx\delta_{1}\delta_{2} which is smaller. δ \delta gap ≍ δ 2 \asymp\delta^{2} Figure 1: Curvature on the unit circle: an arc of proportion δ \delta (central angle 2 ​ π ​ δ 2\pi\delta ) has sagitta 1 − cos ⁡ ( π ​ δ ) ≍ δ 2 1-\cos(\pi\delta)\asymp\delta^{2} between its arc-midpoint and chord-midpoint. Proof. Let s = ( s 1 , ⋯ , s n ) ∈ S ⊂ ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ , ℝ / ℤ ) s=(s_{1},\cdots,s_{n})\in S\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) and pick δ 1 \delta_{1} to be a sufficiently small constant so that 1 / δ 1 1/\delta_{1} is an integer, and let δ 2 = δ 1 5 \delta_{2}=\delta_{1}^{5} be an even smaller constant. In a slight abuse notation, we will use Θ \Theta and O O for quantities that stay constant as δ 1 → 0 \delta_{1}\to 0 , but we will end up making δ 1 \delta_{1} a small enough constant itself. For any x = ( x 1 , ⋯ , x n , ⋯ ) ∈ ℓ 1 ​ ( ℕ , ℝ / ℤ ) x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{n},\cdots)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) , we have that: f ⁡ ( x ) − 2 ​ f ​ ( x + s ) + f ⁡ ( x + 2 ​ s ) = e ⁡ ( x i ) − 2 ​ e ​ ( x i + s i ) + e ⁡ ( x i + 2 ​ s i ) + ∑ j ≠ i ( e ⁡ ( x j ) − 2 ​ e ​ ( x j + s j ) + e ⁡ ( x j + 2 ​ s j ) ) . f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)=e(x_{i})-2e(x_{i}+s_{i})+e(x_{i}+2s_{i})+\sum_{j\neq i}\left(e(x_{j})-2e(x_{j}+s_{j})+e(x_{j}+2s_{j})\right). The first term e ⁡ ( x i ) − 2 ​ e ​ ( x i + s i ) + e ⁡ ( x i + 2 ​ s i ) e(x_{i})-2e(x_{i}+s_{i})+e(x_{i}+2s_{i}) has ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm Θ ⁡ ( δ 1 2 ) \Theta(\delta_{1}^{2}) in ℂ \mathbb{C} while the other terms have a total ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm in ℂ \mathbb{C} of O ⁡ ( ∑ j ≠ i ‖ s j ‖ ℝ / ℤ 2 ) = O ⁡ ( ( max j ≠ i ⁡ ‖ s j ‖ ℝ / ℤ ) ​ ( ∑ j ≠ i ‖ s j ‖ ℝ / ℤ ) ) = O ⁡ ( δ 1 ​ δ 2 ) = O ⁡ ( δ 1 6 ) . O(\sum_{j\neq i}||s_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}^{2})=O\left((\max_{j\neq i}||s_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}})(\sum_{j\neq i}||s_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}})\right)=O(\delta_{1}\delta_{2})=O(\delta_{1}^{6}). So if we choose δ 1 \delta_{1} to be a small enough constant, f ⁡ ( x ) − 2 ​ f ​ ( x + s ) + f ⁡ ( x + 2 ​ s ) f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s) has ℓ 2 \ell^{2} norm Θ ⁡ ( δ 1 2 ) > 2 ​ δ 2 \Theta(\delta^{2}_{1})>2\delta_{2} in ℂ \mathbb{C} . This is less than 1 / 100 1/100 , and is far greater than 2 ​ δ 2 2\delta_{2} as well. This means that f ⁡ ( x ) , f ⁡ ( x + s ) , f ⁡ ( x + 2 ​ s ) f(x),f(x+s),f(x+2s) cannot all be within distance δ 2 \delta_{2} of each other in ℂ / ℤ ⁡ [ i ] \mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Z}[i] , as if they were then f ⁡ ( x ) − 2 ​ f ​ ( x + s ) + f ⁡ ( x + 2 ​ s ) = ( f ⁡ ( x ) − f ⁡ ( x + s )