te number of Bohr sets and nil-Bohr sets. We dealt with the Bohr sets in the last section, and to show that higher-order nil-Bohr sets are not an issue we will use standard results about the independence of higher degree generalized polynomials up to easily handled obstructions. Since S S is constructed only from linear Bohr sets n ​ α {n\alpha} , it is going to generally be independent of n 2 ​ α {n^{2}\alpha} and intersect the set of n n so that ‖ n 2 ​ α ‖ ℝ / ℤ < ε ||n^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}<\varepsilon . Of course n 2 ​ α {n^{2}\alpha} and ( n 2 + n ) ​ α {(n^{2}+n)\alpha} will not be linearly independent of n ​ α {n\alpha} , and so we may get constantly many more linear Bohr obstructions that can arise in this way. Fortunately generalized polynomials can be generated by “basic generalized polynomials” that do equi-distribute unless there is a linear obstruction, so the usual Bohr set obstacles are all we need to check. In particular by the main result of [ 20 ] we may write our generalized polynomials as piecewise polynomial functions of these “basic generalized polynomials” and so any nil-Bohr set contains a nil-Bohr set generated by these basic generalized polynomials. We remark also that the “basic generalized polynomials” of [ 20 ] are slightly different than the “special generalized polynomials” of [ 16 ] , and that Leibman uses the abbreviation “gen-polynomials” for generalized polynomials. We now proceed to a rigorous proof. Let ε i → 0 \varepsilon_{i}\to 0 be a sequence of positive numbers that goes to 0 0 sufficiently quickly, and pick α 1 , α 2 , ⋯ \alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\cdots going to 0 0 sufficiently quickly depending on ε i \varepsilon_{i} . Let S ℕ S_{\mathbb{N}} be the inverse image of S ∞ S_{\infty} under the projection P P . Let B B be a nil-Bohr set. By Theorem 0.2 of [ 20 ] , B B contains a nil-Bohr set generated by basic generalized polynomials B 1 , ⋯ , B k B_{1},\cdots,B_{k} and by Theorem 0.1 of [ 20 ] they jointly equi-distribute. Pick m = m ⁡ ( B ) m=m(B) sufficiently large depending on B B , and consider the map ( P m , B ) (P_{m},B) sending n n to ( { n α 1 } , ⋯ , ⋯ { n α m } , B 1 ( n ) , ⋯ , B k ( n ) ) (\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n)) where k = k ⁡ ( B ) k=k(B) . So we define P m ( n ) = ( { n α 1 } , ⋯ , ⋯ { n α m } ) P_{m}(n)=(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\}) and B ⁡ ( n ) = ( B 1 ​ ( n ) , ⋯ , B k ​ ( n ) ) B(n)=(B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n)) . A subtorus of ( ℝ / ℤ ) m + k (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k} is defined to be the set of points where some set of linear forms with integer coefficients vanish, and the codimension is the number of independent linear forms needed to generate this vanishing set. For our purposes we need a slight modification of Leibman’s result. Theorem 2.7 (Leibman) . Let B = { B 1 , ⋯ , B k } B=\{B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}\} be a family of equi-distributed basic generalized polynomials and let ε > 0 \varepsilon>0 . If α 1 , ⋯ , α m \alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m} are rationally independent irrational numbers, then the image of the map n → ( { n ​ α 1 } , ⋯ , { n ​ α m } , B 1 ​ ( n ) , ⋯ , B k ​ ( n ) ) n\to(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n)) which we denote as ( P m ​ ( n ) , B ​ ( n ) ) (P_{m}(n),B(n)) is ε \varepsilon -dense in some O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) codimension sub-torus of ( ℝ / ℤ ) m + k (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k} . Proof. By Theorem 0.1 of [ 20 ] , the last k k coordinates will equidistribute over the torus ( ℝ / ℤ ) k (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k} . Now reveal the α i \alpha_{i} one by one. At each step, the new α i \alpha_{i} will either be rationally independent from all of our existing gen-polynomials { n ​ β } \{n\beta\} (i.e. the already revealed { n ​ α i } \{n\alpha_{i}\} and the { n ​ β } \{n\beta\} from B B ), in which case { n ​ α i } \{n\alpha_{i}\} will jointly equidistribute, or it will be a rational linear combination of the β \beta . Because the α i \alpha_{i