\alpha_{i} satisfying the conditions of Theorem 2.9 , then for any nil-Bohr set ( B ℓ , ε ℓ ) (B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell}) , P ⁡ ( S ) ∩ B ≠ ∅ P(S)\cap B\neq\emptyset . Proof. Let ε i \varepsilon_{i} be a sequence that goes to 0 0 sufficiently quickly. Choose m m sufficiently large so that S m S_{m} meets all ordinary Bohr sets of complexity O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) . Then as n ∈ ℕ n\in\mathbb{N} ranges, the image ( P m ​ ( n ) , B ​ ( n ) ) (P_{m}(n),B(n)) will intersect S m × ( − ε k , ε k ) k S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k} , because it will be dense in an O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) -codimension subtorus of ( ℝ / ℤ ) m + k (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k} and because S m S_{m} is open in ( ℝ / ℤ ) m (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m} . A priori there is no bound on such n n and it depends on m m as well as the specific choice of O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) -complexity B B . However, by compactness of complexity O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) Nil-Bohr sets ( B ℓ , ε ℓ ) (B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell}) , we can find a uniform bound on n n , and so we can find such an n n sufficiently smaller than 1 / α m + 1 1/\alpha_{m+1} . Hence, we may choose an appropriate sequence of α i \alpha_{i} so that for any nil-Bohr set B B , for sufficiently large m m depending on B B , as n n ranges but stays sufficiently small compared to 1 / α m + 1 1/\alpha_{m+1} , ( P m ​ ( n ) , B ​ ( n ) ) (P_{m}(n),B(n)) intersects S m × ( − ε k , ε k ) k S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k} , because m m was chosen to be sufficiently large so that S m S_{m} meets all ordinary Bohr sets of complexity O k ​ ( 1 ) O_{k}(1) . So we can find some n n so that P m ​ ( n ) ∈ S m P_{m}(n)\in S_{m} and n n is sufficiently smaller than 1 / α k + 1 1/\alpha_{k+1} . By the size bound on n n we have P ⁡ ( n ) ∈ S ∞ P(n)\in S_{\infty} and n ∈ S ℕ n\in S_{\mathbb{N}} . Since the coordinates of B ⁡ ( n ) B(n) have ℝ / ℤ \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z} norm less than ε k \varepsilon_{k} , n n is in the nil-Bohr set B B . This concludes the proof. ∎ A remark about nilmanifolds is in order. We do not use them here, but our results could be phrased equivalently in those terms. Section 2 of [ 20 ] describes the correspondence between equi-distribution of generalized polynomials and the equi-distribution over nilmanifolds from [ 19 ] . The correspondence between generalized polynomials and nilmanifolds was first proved in [ 2 ] and is cited as Lemma 2.3 of [ 20 ] . Theorem 2.9 of [ 8 ] is the quantiative version of Theorem 1.9 of [ 8 ] , the main theorem of [ 19 ] . As written, our construction is not explicit because of the compactness arguments. Using the quantitative results of Green-Tao [ 8 ] , one can remedy this and make all of our compactness arguments effective. 3 Concluding Remarks and Open Questions The most obvious open question here is Katznelson’s question. In view of our disproof of the higher-order version, we conjecture that the answer should be negative. Conjecture 3.1 . There is a set S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} which is a set of Bohr recurrence but not a set of topological recurrence. As usual we also state the combinatorial form of this conjecture. Conjecture 3.2 . There is S ⊂ ℕ S\subset\mathbb{N} so that the Cayley graph on ℤ \mathbb{Z} generated by S S has a finite chromatic number, but does not admit a finite coloring via Bohr sets. One reason to believe Katznelson’s question has a positive answer is that a “zero density exception” version is known due to [ 6 ] . In particular, if A A is a dense subset of ℤ \mathbb{Z} , then S − S S-S contains a 1 − o ⁡ ( 1 ) 1-o(1) proportion of a Bohr set. Katznelson’s question is equivalent to the same claim except with A A syndetic and without the o ⁡ ( 1 ) o(1) proportion, see for instance [ 7 ] . A similar “zero density exception” of the higher-order Katznelson was proved in [ 16 ] , but we have proved here higher-order Katznelson has a negative answer. We generally believe that if Katznelson’s question has a positive answer, the proof somehow relies on topology. Indeed Glasscock, Koutsogiannis, and Richter [ 7 ] prove the special case of skew products over ( ℝ / ℤ ) 2 (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{2} by using the intermediate value theorem, and [ 18 ] relies on the topological lower bound for the chromatic number of the Kneser graph. We also note here that the construction in this paper can be expressed as coming from a skew product over ( ℝ / ℤ ) 5 (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{5} . We mention one important obstacle to extending something like our construction to settle the full Katznelson’s question. Let G G be an Abelian group. Then an element of G m G^{m} in the generating set with sum of coordinates 0 0 can be written in the form ( a 1 − a 2 , a 2 − a 3 , ⋯ , a m − 1 − a m , a m − a 1 ) (a_{1}-a_{2},a_{2}-a_{3},\cdots,a_{m-1}-a_{m},a_{m}-a_{1}) , so the cyclic shifts ( a 1 , a 2 , a 3 , ⋯ , a n ) (a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n}) and ( a 2 , a 3 , ⋯ , a n , a 1 ) (a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n},a_{1}) . Hence, we cannot simply take a function of each coordinate and add it up the way we considered ( e ⁡ ( x ) − 1 ) (e(x)-1) here. This kind of “cyclic shift” obstacle destroys many potential counterexamples to Katznelson’s question, and any kind of high-dimensional projection must break this sort of symmetry. Nonetheless, we believe that our construction represents substantial progress towards Katznelson’s Question. Another interesting direction is to consider the finite field setting. One can also consider analogues of Katznelson’s question over positive characteristic. This was asked by Ben Green over characteristic 2 2 ( [ 9 ] , Problems 52 and 53) and also by John Griesmer [ 10 ] over more general characteristic p p . We can similarly ask the higher-order version of Katznelson’s question in characteristic p p for p > d p>d . We state two such versions for 3 3 -term progressions. Question 3.3 . Let p 1 , p 2 , ⋯ , p n , ⋯ ≥ 3 p_{1},p_{2},\cdots,p_{n},\cdots\geq 3 be an increasing sequence of primes, and say we have a sequence G i G_{i} of Cayley graphs on ℤ / p i \mathbb{Z}/p_{i} with uniformly bounded chromatic number C C . Is there a uniform constant K K so that the generating set of G i G_{i} is contained in the union of K K sets ‖ P j ​ ( x ) / p ‖ ℝ / ℤ > 1 / K ||P_{j}(x)/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}>1/K , where P j P_{j} are polynomials on ℤ / p i \mathbb{Z}/p_{i} of degree at most 2 2 ? In other words, can we always find a bounded number K K of quadratics P j ​ ( x ) P_{j}(x) so that any x x in the generating set is mapped by at least one of the quadratics to a point that is not in the interval ( − p / K , p / K ) (-p/K,p/K) ? Question 3.4 . Let p ≥ 3 p\geq 3 be a fixed prime, and say we have a sequence of Cayley graphs G i G_{i} on ( ℤ / p ) n (\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n} for a sequence of n = n i n=n_{i} with uniformly bounded chromatic number C C . Is there a uniform constant K K so that the generating set of G i G_{i} is contained in the union of K K sets P j ​ ( x ) ≠ 0 P_{j}(x)\neq 0 , where P j P_{j} are quadratic forms on ( ℤ / p ) n (\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n} of degree at most 2 2 ? A few comments are in order about why our example does not immediately resolve the above questions. Our example in some sense relies on the infinite bracket polynomial ∑ i { n ​ a i } 2 \sum_{i}\{na_{i}\}^{2} . In modulo 1 1 , x x and x 2 x^{2} are independent. However, in modulo p p the value of n n determines the value of n 2 n^{2} , and ∑ i ( n ​ a i mod p ) 2 = n 2 ​ ( ∑ a i 2 ) mod p \sum_{i}(na_{i}\mod p)^{2}=n^{2}(\sum a_{i}^{2})\mod p . Our example also relies heavily on the existence of a norm, which is less straightforward in positive characteristic, though one can consider the p p -adic norm or ‖ x / p ‖ ℝ / ℤ ||x/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} . In general, it is also true that ℤ / p \mathbb{Z}/p and ℝ / ℤ \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z} “look different” from the standpoint of arithmetic progressions of length 3 3 or greater. See [ 4 ] for discussion about this. One can also ask if any S S which is not a set of nil-Bohr has some kind of “quadratic structure” . We generally suspect the answer is no, and in fact that we do not think it is possible to write down a “nice” set of criteria that determines whether S S is a set of multiple recurrence or if S S is a set of single recurrence. There is likely some logical or computability theoretic separation between nil-Bohr recurrence and multiple recurrence, as well as between Bohr recurrence and topological recurrence. We state a complexity theoretic conjecture in the finitary setting, which implies that the answer to Katznelson’s Question is negative in a strong sense. One could formulate a similar finitary conjecture for the higher-order version. Conjecture 3.5 . There exists an absolute constant c c so that for every constant C C , it is N ​ P NP -hard to distinguish if an Abelian Cayley Graph that has chromatic number ≤ c \leq c or ≥ C \geq C . For an Abelian Cayley graph, we can determine in polynomial time whether there exists a coloring by Bohr sets using < C <C colors by simply checking all such colorings, and in [ 17 ] it is proved that Katznelson’s Question is equivalent to the statement that the number of colors necessary in such a coloring is bounded a function of the chromatic number. Hence, if Katznelson’s Question has a positive answer, there is a polynomial algorithm for this “Approximate Graph Coloring” question for Abelian Cayley graphs. For general graphs, such problems are known to be NP-hard conditional on a variant of the Unique Games Conjecture, which gives us reason to believe Conjecture 3.5 . See [ 5 ] and the references there for more discussion of Approximate Graph Coloring. We do not make any attempt at all to optimize the number of colors used in our construction. With our ideas it seems difficult to beat the bounds from the unit distance in the plane. Let us also mention that while it is simple to check that at least 3 3 colors are necessary for any example answering Katznelson’s Question in the negative [ 7 ] , in this setting of 3 3 -term and longer progressions we do not even know if we can have a counterexample using only 2 2 colors. Concerning counterexamples with only 2 2 colors, we also mention here the 2 2 -large versus large conjecture of Brown, Graham, and Landman [ 3 ] . A set S S is large if it is a set of d d -topological recurrence for all d d , so that restricted van der Waerden with restricted common difference s ∈ S s\in S is true for arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions. A set S S is 2-large if any 2-coloring of ℕ \mathbb{N} has arbitrarily large arithmetic progressions with common difference in S S . Brown, Graham, and Landman asked if 2 2 -large sets are large. We have constructed the first example of a set that is not large but meets every nil-Bohr set. We conjecture that it is necessary to do so to refute the 2 2 -large versus large conjecture. Conjecture 3.6 . If S S is not a set of d d -nilBohr recurrence for some d d , then S S is not 2 2 -large. We also ask whether these sets, or some similarly constructed modifications, are 2 2 -large. Question 3.7 . Are the sets S S of the kind constructed here 2 2 -large? Another potential question we can ask is about the polynomial version of Katznelson’s question. What if we consider the Bergelson-Leibman polynomial van der Waerden Theorem [ 1 ] with restricted differences? We pose one such question. Question 3.8 . For what sets S S does an arbitrary finite coloring of ℕ \mathbb{N} always contain a monochromatic { x , x + d , x + d 2 } \{x,x+d,x+d^{2}\} with d ∈ S d\in S ? Progress on any of these questions would be interesting and would contribute to our general understanding of recurrence. 4 Acknowledgments We thank GPT-5, Zachary Chase, Yang P. Liu, and Siming Tu for helpful comments. References [1] V. Bergelson and A. Leibman, Polynomial extensions of van der Waerden’s and Szemerédi’s theorems, J. Amer. Math. Soc. 9 (1996), no. 3, 725–753. MR1325795. https://doi.org/10